WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
On the left, a smooth 60 frames per second scroll.

2
00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.920
On the right, a radic stuttering.

3
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:09.360
Overlaying the Perfetto choreographer lane reveals why.

4
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.200
The left hits consistent 16.6 millisecond intervals.

5
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.400
The right shows elongated sections missing the deadline,

6
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.160
dropping frames entirely.

7
00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.040
The instant for many developers is to immediately start

8
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.200
refactoring code to fix the stutter,

9
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.400
but the cardinal rule of performance engineering is simple.

10
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.040
Do not optimize what you have not measured.

11
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.840
Dropped frames are trailing indicators of a bottleneck,

12
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.840
typically located in the JavaScript thread,

13
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.000
or the JS to native bridge.

14
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.440
Isolating that bottleneck requires a strict diagnostic loop.

15
00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.640
You form a hypothesis, measure it with a profiling tool,

16
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.400
apply a targeted fix, and measure again to verify impact.

17
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.920
Bypassing this empirical process means you are coding blind,

18
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.440
guessing its structural flaws without data rarely fixes the problem

19
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.560
and usually introduces entirely new performance regressions

20
00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.000
into the application.

21
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.320
This react native component tree updates via a strict contract.

22
00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.480
Rule one, a component re-renders when its local state changes.

23
00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.560
Rule two, when apparent updates,

24
00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.000
a chain reaction forces every connected child to re-render,

25
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.400
unless protected by react.memo.

26
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.600
Rule three states that a component will re-render anytime

27
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.560
a global context that it consumes receives an update.

28
00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.120
And rule four triggers a re-render whenever a hook

29
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.320
the component relies on returns a new reference in memory.

30
00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.720
Internalizing this precise designing architecture

31
00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.000
that intentionally opts out of unnecessary compute cycles,

32
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.800
you protect the UI thread from being overwhelmed.

33
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.280
When you suspect that JavaScript execution itself

34
00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.880
is dragging down your frame rate,

35
00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.720
the primary diagnostic instrument is the React Dev Tools profiler.

36
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.760
Looking at a profiler flame chart tracks commit times.

37
00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:50.080
To diagnose issues, enable record why each component rendered

38
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:53.360
to isolate the exact prop forcing the commit.

39
00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.040
This view frequently exposes a common architectural flaw,

40
00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.840
the Redux Toolkit Uselector trap.

41
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:03.120
This cascade happens because every Uselector call

42
00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.960
re-evaluates on every single dispatch to the global store.

43
00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.640
Even if a component's specific slice of data remains identical,

44
00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.560
the hook can return a new memory reference, triggering rule four.

45
00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.520
Moving to ZooStand addresses this specific overhead

46
00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.400
by changing how components interact with the store.

47
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.880
ZooStand avoids the global dispatch trap

48
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.200
through an architecture based on fine-grained

49
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.120
per selector subscriptions.

50
00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.320
A component only recalculates when the exact piece of state

51
00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.560
it points to actually changes.

52
00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.800
Moving from global dispatch evaluations

53
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.920
to localized subscriptions prevents accidental contract

54
00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.520
violations at scale.

55
00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.640
This ensures the JavaScript thread remains completely idle

56
00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.320
until a targeted update absolutely requires its processing power.

57
00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.240
Sometimes the JavaScript code itself is highly optimized,

58
00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.800
yet the application still drops frames.

59
00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.080
In those cases, the friction lies at the complex boundary

60
00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.280
where JavaScript communicates with native modules.

61
00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.520
This is a Perfetto timeline, mapping asynchronous operations.

62
00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.240
When investigating slow native view creation,

63
00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:09.040
we isolate the fabric.comit track.

64
00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.160
Reading this commit lane tracks how long the shadow tree takes

65
00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:13.840
to calculate layouts.

66
00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.600
The JSI category track specifically exposes host function

67
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:18.880
call durations.

68
00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:21.920
It measures exactly how many milliseconds are lost,

69
00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.120
waiting for data to cross the bridge between environments.

70
00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:27.920
A common reaction to deep execution delays

71
00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:30.720
is defensively wrapping every JavaScript component

72
00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.080
in a use memo hook, hoping to cache rendering output.

73
00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.120
But memoization has a hidden execution cost.

74
00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.560
The engine is still forced to run a dependency array comparison

75
00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.040
on every single render cycle to determine

76
00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.120
if the cache value remains valid.

77
00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.920
Use memo is not a free performance boost.

78
00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:49.840
It only yields a net positive

79
00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.080
when the raw computation being bypassed

80
00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:53.920
is heavier than the processing cost

81
00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:56.000
of the dependency comparison.

82
00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.920
Another hidden tax on the JS thread comes from memory allocation.

83
00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.480
Continuously mounting and unmounting elements

84
00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:04.160
triggers garbage collection.

85
00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:05.840
This forces the JavaScript thread

86
00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.880
to aggressively halt all of its primary operations,

87
00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.480
freezing the interface completely

88
00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.560
while it frantically queens up memory.

89
00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.040
Left, flat list eagerly generates

90
00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:17.440
and destroys UI elements off screen.

91
00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:20.400
Right, flash list uses a fixed pool of items,

92
00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.720
looping back to the bottom to swap data.

93
00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:24.560
Because containers are recycled,

94
00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:27.840
an accurate, estimated item size property is critical.

95
00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:29.520
Otherwise, the engine miscalculates,

96
00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:31.440
causing jarring layout jumps.

97
00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.120
This logic of pooling resources applies directly to image assets.

98
00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.200
An un-cashed image forces the device

99
00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.080
to run an expensive re-download and re-decode operation

100
00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.160
every time the interface renders.

101
00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.680
You bypass this by implementing strict disk and memory

102
00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.920
caching, using libraries like fast image or expo image.

103
00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:51.920
For assets that rarely change,

104
00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:54.400
you use content-addressed URLs,

105
00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.880
allowing the URL string itself to function

106
00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:58.800
as a permanent cache key.

107
00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.200
The final layer of this caching architecture

108
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.200
extends to your network requests,

109
00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.680
using tools like TanStack Query.

110
00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.520
TanStack Query operates on a stale-while-revalidate model.

111
00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.320
If a user navigates back to a visited screen,

112
00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.800
the UI renders instantly from cache,

113
00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.920
silently refetching fresh data in the background.

114
00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:21.920
By recycling UI views and aggressively caching external assets,

115
00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:25.120
you effectively starve the garbage collector of work.

116
00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.480
This guarantees that the JavaScript thread remains unblocked

117
00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:32.080
and fully responsive during intense user interactions.

118
00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.280
Optimization requires aligning the rendering contract

119
00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:39.200
with engine capabilities through rigorous empirical measurement.

120
00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.960
This tool selection matrix maps your specific hypothesis

121
00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:46.000
to the exact diagnostic instrument required.

122
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.880
Suspect as script is dragging down frame rates.

123
00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:53.280
Hermes isolates that JSCPU hotspot into a flame chart.

124
00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:54.800
Data loading sluggish,

125
00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.240
flipper maps out the exact request waterfall timings,

126
00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:00.400
strictly enforcing the rendering contract

127
00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.400
and applying the correct diagnostic tool

128
00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.080
to each suspected layer,

129
00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.880
allows developers to maintain smooth frame timings

130
00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:10.400
and predictable behavior across complex architectures.

